export default "# CSS Properties, Values and Units\n\n*A four-part beginner lesson. Running example: building a small \"style guide\" page for Harbor Goods, an online shop that sells outdoor and boating gear. Each experiment shows how one property, value, or unit behaves.*\n\n---\n\n## Part 1 of 4: Properties and Values\n\n### 1.1 The big idea in one minute\n\nCSS is a list of instructions that tells the browser how things should look. Every instruction has two halves:\n\n- A **property**: *what* you want to change (for example, the text colour).\n- A **value**: *what you want it to be* (for example, blue).\n\nThink of a form with fields. The property is the label (\"Text colour\"), and the value is what you write in the box (\"blue\").\n\n```css\np {\n  color: blue;\n  font-size: 1.25rem;\n}\n```\n\n### 1.2 The parts of a declaration\n\nOne instruction (a property plus its value) is called a **declaration**.\n\n```css\ncolor: blue;\n```\n\n| Part | In the example | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| Property | `color` | Says what to change |\n| Colon | `:` | Separates the property from the value |\n| Value | `blue` | Says what to change it to |\n| Semicolon | `;` | Marks the end of the instruction |\n\nA group of declarations inside curly braces `{ }`, together with the **selector** that says which element they apply to, is called a **rule**.\n\n```css\nh1 {              /* selector: which element? */\n  color: navy;    /* declaration 1 */\n  font-size: 2rem;/* declaration 2 */\n}\n```\n\nAlways finish each declaration with a semicolon. If you forget one, the browser may read two instructions as one, and both can stop working.\n\n### 1.3 Each property accepts only certain values\n\nYou cannot put just anything after the colon. Each property only understands certain kinds of value.\n\n| This works | This does not work | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| `display: flex;` | `display: 1rem;` | `display` wants a keyword, not a size |\n| `padding: 1rem;` | `padding: flex;` | `padding` wants a size, not a keyword |\n| `color: #0b3d5c;` | `color: 20px;` | `color` wants a colour, not a size |\n\nIf you give a property a value it does not understand, the browser **ignores that one instruction** and carries on. It does not show an error on the page. This is why a typo can be hard to spot: nothing happens, and nothing complains.\n\n### 1.4 The kinds of value you will meet\n\n| Kind of value | Looks like | Example use |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Keyword** (a word) | `bold`, `center`, `none`, `block`, `auto` | `font-weight: bold;` |\n| **Number** | `1.5`, `0.8`, `3` | `line-height: 1.5;`, `opacity: 0.8;` |\n| **Length** (a number plus a unit) | `16px`, `1.5rem`, `50%` | `width: 20rem;` |\n| **Colour** | `#0b3d5c`, `rgb(11 61 92)`, `tomato` | `color: tomato;` |\n| **Text in quotes** (a string) | `\"Harbor\"` | `content: \"→\";` |\n| **File address** (a URL) | `url(\"images/hero.jpg\")` | `background-image: url(\"images/hero.jpg\");` |\n| **Time** | `0.2s`, `200ms` | `transition-duration: 0.2s;` |\n| **Angle** | `45deg` | `transform: rotate(45deg);` |\n| **Function** (a value that does a calculation) | `calc(100% - 2rem)`, `var(--blue)` | `width: calc(100% - 2rem);` |\n| **List** (values separated by commas) | `Arial, sans-serif` | `font-family: Arial, sans-serif;` |\n\n### 1.5 Where do I look up what a property accepts?\n\nYou do not need to memorise hundreds of properties. Use **MDN Web Docs** (developer.mozilla.org). Search for the property name, for example \"MDN padding\". Each page has:\n\n- A short explanation and examples you can edit live.\n- A **Syntax** section listing the allowed values.\n- A **Browser compatibility** table showing which browsers support it.\n\nA few symbols you will see in the syntax box:\n\n| Symbol | Plain meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `\\|` | \"or\": pick **one** of these |\n| `{1,4}` | Repeat 1 to 4 times (for example `padding` takes one to four values) |\n| `<length>` | Any size with a unit |\n| `<color>` | Any colour |\n\n### 1.6 Five special keywords that work on every property\n\n| Keyword | Plain meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `inherit` | \"Use whatever my parent element has\" |\n| `initial` | \"Go back to the default from the CSS standard\" |\n| `unset` | \"Inherit if this property normally inherits, otherwise use the default\" |\n| `revert` | \"Go back to what the browser would have done without my styles\" |\n| `revert-layer` | A more advanced version of `revert` that you can ignore for now |\n\nA handy use: buttons do not copy the page's font by default. This one line fixes that:\n\n```css\nbutton { font: inherit; }\n```\n\n### 1.7 Shorthand: one line that sets many things\n\nSome properties are a **shorthand** for several smaller ones. They save typing.\n\n| Shorthand | Sets (the smaller properties) | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| `margin` | The space outside on all four sides | `margin: 1rem;` |\n| `padding` | The space inside on all four sides | `padding: 1rem 2rem;` |\n| `border` | Width, style, and colour | `border: 2px solid red;` |\n| `font` | Style, weight, size, line height, family | `font: italic 700 1rem/1.6 Arial, sans-serif;` |\n| `background` | Colour, image, position, size, repeat | `background: #eee url(\"a.png\") no-repeat;` |\n| `gap` | Row gap and column gap | `gap: 1rem 2rem;` |\n\nThe smaller properties are called **longhand**: `margin-top`, `margin-right`, `margin-bottom`, `margin-left`.\n\n**The four-value order.** When a shorthand takes up to four values for the four sides, they go **clockwise, starting at the top**: top, right, bottom, left. A way to remember: \"TRouBLe\".\n\n| You write | Top | Right | Bottom | Left |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `padding: 1rem;` | 1rem | 1rem | 1rem | 1rem |\n| `padding: 1rem 2rem;` | 1rem | 2rem | 1rem | 2rem |\n| `padding: 1rem 2rem 3rem;` | 1rem | 2rem | 3rem | 2rem |\n| `padding: 1rem 2rem 3rem 4rem;` | 1rem | 2rem | 3rem | 4rem |\n\n**Three shorthand traps for beginners:**\n\n1. **A shorthand resets everything you leave out.** If you write `font: 1rem Arial;`, the browser also sets the font weight, style, and line height back to their defaults. Any `line-height` you wrote *before* it is wiped out.\n2. **Some parts are required.** `border: 3px red;` shows **no border at all**, because you left out the style (`solid`, `dashed`, and so on), and the default style is `none`.\n3. **Order sometimes matters.** In `font`, the size must come before the family.\n\n### 1.8 Writing values correctly\n\n| Rule | Right | Wrong |\n|---|---|---|\n| No space between the number and its unit | `16px`, `1.5rem` | `16 px`, `1.5 rem` |\n| Sizes need a unit (zero is the only exception) | `margin: 10px;` `margin: 0;` | `margin: 10;` |\n| Times need a unit, even zero | `0s`, `0.2s` | `0` |\n| Spaces around `+` and `-` inside `calc()` | `calc(100% - 2rem)` | `calc(100%-2rem)` |\n| Put quotes around font names that have spaces | `\"Segoe UI\"` | `Segoe UI` |\n| Use a decimal point, not a comma | `1.5rem` | `1,5rem` |\n| `!important` goes after the value | `color: red !important;` | `!important color: red;` |\n\n### 1.9 If a value is wrong\n\nThe browser **throws away only that one declaration** and keeps going. This is useful: you can write a safe value first and a newer one after it. An older browser skips the one it does not understand.\n\n```css\n.box {\n  width: 50%;                /* safe value */\n  width: calc(50% - 1rem);   /* better value; used if the browser understands it */\n}\n```\n\n**Part 1 summary.** You can name the parts of a declaration, list the kinds of value, look up a property on MDN, use the special keywords, read shorthand in the clockwise order, avoid the three shorthand traps, and write values correctly.\n\n---\n\n## Part 2 of 4: Units\n\nA **unit** says *how big* a number is. `16` alone means nothing, while `16px` or `16rem` are very different sizes.\n\n### 2.1 Two families of units\n\n| Family | What it means | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Absolute** | A fixed size that does not change with anything else | `px`, `cm`, `mm`, `in`, `pt` |\n| **Relative** | A size that depends on something else (the text size, the screen size, the parent's size) | `rem`, `em`, `%`, `vw`, `vh`, `ch` |\n\nRelative units are the secret of pages that look good on every screen and for every reader.\n\n### 2.2 `px` (pixels)\n\nA **CSS pixel** is a tiny fixed unit. On a phone or a sharp laptop screen it is not one physical dot, but it always keeps the same proportions.\n\n- **Good for:** thin borders (`1px`), small shadows, and fine details.\n- **Not good for:** font sizes, because a fixed pixel size ignores a person's choice of bigger text in their browser.\n\n### 2.3 `rem` (the best choice for most sizes)\n\n`rem` means \"**r**oot **em**\". It is a multiple of the **root font size**, which is the text size set on the `<html>` element. In almost every browser that is **16px** unless the user has changed it.\n\n| You write | In pixels (root = 16px) |\n|---|---|\n| `1rem` | 16px |\n| `1.5rem` | 24px |\n| `2rem` | 32px |\n| `0.5rem` | 8px |\n| `0.75rem` | 12px |\n\n**Why it is great:** if a visitor sets their browser to \"large text\" (say 20px), every `rem` size grows with it automatically. Your page respects their choice.\n\n**Beginner tip:** do not change the root size to pixels (`html { font-size: 16px; }`). That would switch off this feature.\n\n### 2.4 `em` (a size based on the text around it)\n\n`em` is like `rem`, but it is based on the **current element's text size** (or the parent's, when used in `font-size`).\n\n- **Good for:** spacing that should grow with its own text, such as a button's padding: `padding: 0.5em 1em;`.\n- **The trap:** `em` in `font-size` **multiplies layer by layer** when elements are nested.\n\nIf every nested box says `font-size: 1.2em`, and the root is 16px:\n\n| Nesting level | Calculation | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Level 1 | 16 × 1.2 | 19.2px |\n| Level 2 | 19.2 × 1.2 | 23.04px |\n| Level 3 | 23.04 × 1.2 | 27.648px |\n\nThe text keeps getting bigger! With `rem`, every level would be exactly 19.2px. **Rule of thumb: use `rem` for font sizes, and use `em` only when you want something to follow its own text size.**\n\n### 2.5 `%` (percent): \"percent of what?\"\n\nA percentage is always a percentage **of something else**, and what that something is depends on the property.\n\n| Property | `%` means a percentage of... |\n|---|---|\n| `width` | The **width** of the parent box |\n| `height` | The **height** of the parent (only works if the parent has a set height) |\n| `font-size` | The parent's font size |\n| `padding` and `margin` (all four sides) | The parent's **width** (even for top and bottom!) |\n| `border-radius` | The element's own size (`50%` makes a circle from a square) |\n\nThe padding rule surprises almost everyone. In a parent that is 400px wide, `padding: 10%` gives **40px on all four sides**, because 10% of 400 is 40, even at the top and bottom.\n\n### 2.6 Screen-size units: `vw` and `vh`\n\n| Unit | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `1vw` | 1% of the **width** of the browser window |\n| `1vh` | 1% of the **height** of the browser window |\n| `vmin` / `vmax` | 1% of the smaller or larger of the two |\n| `dvh` | Like `vh`, but adjusts for the address bar that appears and hides on phones |\n\nTwo beginner warnings:\n\n1. **`100vw` can cause a sideways scrollbar**, because it counts the width of the scrollbar too. For \"full width\", use `width: 100%` or let blocks fill their container by default.\n2. **`100vh` on phones** can be taller than what you can see. Where it matters, use `100dvh`.\n\n### 2.7 `ch` (the width of a character)\n\n`1ch` is roughly the width of the number zero in the current font. It is perfect for **comfortable line lengths**. About 45 to 75 characters per line is easiest to read:\n\n```css\np { max-width: 65ch; }\n```\n\n### 2.8 `fr` (a share of the free space)\n\n`fr` is only used in **CSS Grid**. It means \"a fraction of the space left over\".\n\n```css\n.layout { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr; }\n```\n\nThe second column gets twice as much space as the first.\n\n### 2.9 Numbers with no unit\n\nA few properties want a plain number, and adding a unit would be wrong or would change the meaning.\n\n| Property | Example | What the number means |\n|---|---|---|\n| `line-height` | `1.6` | A multiplier of the text size (recommended) |\n| `opacity` | `0.8` | 0 is invisible, 1 is fully solid |\n| `z-index` | `3` | Which element sits on top (a whole number) |\n| `font-weight` | `700` | How bold (100 to 900) |\n| `flex-grow` | `1` | A share of the spare space |\n\n**Unitless `line-height` versus a percentage.** This is a small detail that saves a lot of trouble.\n\n| You write on the parent (text size 20px) | What a child with 40px text gets |\n|---|---|\n| `line-height: 1.5` | 40 × 1.5 = **60px** (the number is passed on and recalculated) |\n| `line-height: 150%` | **30px** (the parent worked out 30px and passed on that fixed size, so the big child's lines are cramped) |\n\nAlways write `line-height` as a plain number.\n\n### 2.10 Doing sums in CSS: `calc()`, `min()`, `max()`, `clamp()`\n\n| Function | What it does | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| `calc()` | Does arithmetic, even mixing units | `width: calc(100% - 2rem);` |\n| `min()` | Picks the **smaller** value (so it works like a maximum limit) | `width: min(100%, 40rem);` |\n| `max()` | Picks the **larger** value (so it works like a minimum limit) | `font-size: max(1rem, 2vw);` |\n| `clamp(smallest, ideal, largest)` | Keeps a value between two limits | `font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 1rem + 3vw, 3rem);` |\n\n**Remember:** inside `calc()` you must put spaces around `+` and `-`. (`calc(100%-2rem)` does not work.)\n\n**How `clamp()` works for a heading.** `clamp(1.75rem, 1rem + 3vw, 3rem)` means \"aim for `1rem + 3vw`, but never go below 28px, and never above 48px.\" On a narrow phone the heading stays at 28px. On a wide monitor it stops growing at 48px. In between it grows smoothly. This is called **fluid typography**.\n\n### 2.11 Colours\n\nThere are several ways to write the same colour. They all work, and you can use whichever you like.\n\n| Format | Example | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Name** | `tomato`, `navy`, `white` | About 140 names |\n| **Hex code** | `#0b3d5c` | A `#` plus six digits (red, green, blue). Short form `#fff` for repeating pairs |\n| **`rgb()`** | `rgb(11 61 92)` | Red, green, blue, each from 0 to 255 |\n| **`hsl()`** | `hsl(203 79% 20%)` | Hue (the colour wheel angle 0 to 360), saturation (how vivid), lightness (how light) |\n| **With transparency** | `rgb(11 61 92 / 50%)` | The part after the slash is \"alpha\": 0% is invisible, 100% is solid |\n| **`transparent`** | `transparent` | Fully see-through |\n| **`currentColor`** | `border-color: currentColor;` | \"Use my current text colour\" |\n\nWhich property uses colour?\n\n| Property | Colours... |\n|---|---|\n| `color` | The **text** |\n| `background-color` | The **background** |\n| `border-color` | The **border** |\n| `outline-color` | The outline (focus ring) |\n| `accent-color` | Native checkboxes, radio buttons, and sliders |\n\nTwo useful differences:\n\n- **`opacity`** fades the **whole element**, including its text. A colour with an alpha value (`rgb(0 0 0 / 50%)`) fades only that colour.\n- **`hsl()`** is easy for making lighter and darker versions: keep the hue, change the lightness.\n\n**Colour and readability.** Text needs enough **contrast** against its background so people can read it. The standard minimum is a ratio of **4.5 to 1** for normal text (3 to 1 for large text). In the browser's developer tools, click the little colour square next to a colour to see the ratio. Also, **never use colour as the only clue**: a red error needs words or an icon too, because some people cannot tell red from green.\n\n### 2.12 Custom properties (your own variables)\n\nA **custom property** (also called a CSS variable) is a name that you choose for a value, so you can reuse it. Names start with two hyphens.\n\n```css\n:root {\n  --blue: #0b3d5c;\n  --space: 1rem;\n}\n\n.card {\n  color: var(--blue);\n  padding: var(--space);\n}\n```\n\n| Part | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `:root` | The top of the page, so the variable is available everywhere |\n| `--blue: #0b3d5c;` | Define the variable |\n| `var(--blue)` | Use the variable |\n| `var(--blue, navy)` | Use `--blue`, but fall back to `navy` if it is missing |\n\nWhy use them: change `--blue` in one place and every use updates. Variable names are **case-sensitive** (`--Blue` is not `--blue`).\n\nA gotcha: if the variable holds something the property cannot use (for example `--space: banana`), the property loses its value completely (it acts as if you had not set it), rather than using an earlier rule.\n\n**Part 2 summary.** You know the absolute and relative unit families, why `rem` is the best default for text, how the `em` trap works, what each percentage is a percentage of, how `vw`, `vh`, `ch`, and `fr` work, when to use plain numbers, how to use `calc()`, `min()`, `max()`, and `clamp()`, how to write colours, and how to use custom properties.\n\n---\n\n## Part 3 of 4: Properties You Will Use Every Day\n\n### 3.1 Properties grouped by job\n\n| Job | Common properties |\n|---|---|\n| **Size and space** | `width`, `height`, `max-width`, `margin`, `padding`, `box-sizing` |\n| **Borders and shapes** | `border`, `border-radius`, `outline`, `box-shadow` |\n| **Text** | `font-family`, `font-size`, `font-weight`, `line-height`, `text-align`, `letter-spacing` |\n| **Colour and background** | `color`, `background-color`, `background-image`, `opacity` |\n| **Layout** | `display`, `position`, `flex`, `grid-template-columns`, `gap` |\n| **Movement** | `transform`, `transition`, `animation` |\n| **Mouse and scrolling** | `cursor`, `scroll-behavior` |\n\n### 3.2 The box: size and space\n\nEvery element is a rectangular box with four layers, from the inside out:\n\n```text\n+--------------------------------+\n|             margin             |   space OUTSIDE the box\n|   +------------------------+   |\n|   |         border         |   |   the edge line\n|   |   +----------------+   |   |\n|   |   |    padding     |   |   |   space INSIDE the border\n|   |   |   [ content ]  |   |   |   your text or image\n|   |   +----------------+   |   |\n|   +------------------------+   |\n+--------------------------------+\n```\n\n| Property | Plain meaning | Beginner note |\n|---|---|---|\n| `width`, `height` | The size of the content area | Prefer `max-width` for flexible pages |\n| `padding` | Breathing room **inside** the border | Takes the background colour |\n| `border` | The edge | Needs `width style color`, for example `1px solid #ccc` |\n| `margin` | Space **outside** the border | Always see-through; neighbouring vertical margins can merge |\n| `box-sizing` | Whether `width` counts padding and border | `border-box` is friendlier |\n\n**The width surprise and its fix.** By default, if you set `width: 200px` and `padding: 20px`, the box ends up **240px wide** (the padding is added on top). Almost every project starts with this fix, which makes `width` include padding and border:\n\n```css\n*, *::before, *::after {\n  box-sizing: border-box;\n}\n```\n\nOther handy sizing properties:\n\n| Property | What it does | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| `max-width` | The box can shrink but never grow past this | `max-width: 60rem;` |\n| `min-height` | The box is at least this tall, but grows if needed | `min-height: 10rem;` |\n| `margin: 0 auto` | Centres a block that has a set width | `margin: 0 auto;` |\n| `aspect-ratio` | Keeps width-to-height proportion | `aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;` |\n| `border-radius` | Rounds corners | `border-radius: 0.5rem;` (`50%` makes a circle) |\n| `box-shadow` | Adds a shadow | `box-shadow: 0 2px 6px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.15);` |\n\nUse **`min-height`**, not `height`, for boxes with text. A fixed `height` makes text spill out when someone enlarges the text size.\n\n### 3.3 Text\n\n| Property | What it does | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| `font-family` | Which typeface, with backups | `font-family: system-ui, \"Segoe UI\", Arial, sans-serif;` |\n| `font-size` | How big | `font-size: 1rem;` |\n| `font-weight` | How bold | `400` normal, `700` bold |\n| `font-style` | Italic or not | `italic` |\n| `line-height` | Space between lines | `1.6` (a plain number) |\n| `text-align` | Left, centre, right | `text-align: center;` |\n| `text-decoration` | Underline and similar | `text-decoration: none;` |\n| `text-transform` | Changes capitals without changing the text | `uppercase` |\n| `letter-spacing` | Space between letters | `0.05em` |\n\n**Always give font backups.** A list like `system-ui, \"Segoe UI\", Arial, sans-serif` means \"try the first; if not available, try the next.\" End with a generic family (`sans-serif`, `serif`, or `monospace`) so there is always something. Fonts with spaces in their names go in quotes.\n\n### 3.4 Colour, background, and opacity\n\n| Property | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `color` | Text colour (passes down to child elements) |\n| `background-color` | Background colour |\n| `background-image` | A picture or a gradient: `linear-gradient(135deg, #0b3d5c, #1d6f9f)` |\n| `background-size` | `cover` (fill the area, may crop) or `contain` (fit inside) |\n| `opacity` | Fades the whole element (0 to 1) |\n\nUse a CSS `background-image` for **decoration** only. Because it has no alt text, a picture that carries meaning belongs in an `<img>` tag with `alt` instead.\n\n### 3.5 Layout\n\n| Property | Common values | Plain meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `display` | `block`, `inline`, `flex`, `grid`, `none` | How the element takes part in the layout. `none` removes it completely, including for screen readers |\n| `gap` | `1rem` | Space between items in a flex or grid layout |\n| `justify-content` | `center`, `space-between` | Position items along the main direction (flex) |\n| `align-items` | `center`, `stretch` | Position items across the other direction (flex) |\n| `position` | `relative`, `absolute`, `fixed`, `sticky` | Moves an element out of its normal spot |\n\n### 3.6 Movement\n\n```css\n.button {\n  transition: background-color 0.2s ease, transform 0.2s ease;\n}\n.button:hover { background-color: #ffc233; }\n.button:active { transform: translateY(1px); }\n```\n\n| Property | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `transition` | Makes a change happen smoothly over time (property, duration, easing) |\n| `transform` | Moves, rotates, or scales without disturbing the layout |\n| `animation` | Plays a multi-step animation defined with `@keyframes` |\n\nTips: always include the **unit on times** (`0.2s`, not `0.2`), name the exact properties instead of writing `transition: all`, and animate `transform` and `opacity` because they are the smoothest.\n\n### 3.7 Logical properties (a small bonus)\n\nWords like `left` and `right` assume left-to-right text. In languages such as Arabic and Hebrew, text runs right-to-left. **Logical properties** follow the reading direction automatically.\n\n| Physical | Logical (flips automatically) |\n|---|---|\n| `margin-left` | `margin-inline-start` |\n| `padding-right` | `padding-inline-end` |\n| `border-left` | `border-inline-start` |\n| `width` | `inline-size` |\n| `text-align: left` | `text-align: start` |\n\nYou can learn these gradually, but `margin-inline: auto` and `padding-block: 1rem` are easy to start with.\n\n### 3.8 Choosing the right unit\n\n| For this | Use | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Font sizes | `rem` | Follows the visitor's text-size setting |\n| Line height | A plain number such as `1.6` | Passes down correctly |\n| Space around text (padding, margin) | `rem` | Scales with text |\n| Space inside a button that should follow its own text size | `em` | Tied to that element's text |\n| Line length | `ch` | `max-width: 65ch` |\n| Thin borders and small shadows | `px` | Fixed fine detail |\n| Page and column widths | `%`, `fr`, `max-width` | Flexible |\n| Full-height sections on phones | `dvh` | Accounts for the address bar |\n| Headings that scale smoothly | `clamp()` | Safe minimum and maximum |\n| Times | `s` or `ms` | Required |\n| Print stylesheets | `pt`, `cm`, `mm` | Physical sizes |\n\n### 3.9 Inheritance: what gets passed down\n\nSome properties are automatically **passed from a parent to its children**, and some are not.\n\n| Usually passed down (inherited) | Usually **not** passed down |\n|---|---|\n| `color` | `margin`, `padding`, `border` |\n| `font-family`, `font-size`, `font-weight` | `background` |\n| `line-height`, `letter-spacing` | `width`, `height` |\n| `text-align` | `display`, `position` |\n| `cursor`, `visibility` | `opacity`, `transform` |\n| Custom properties (`--blue`) | `box-shadow`, `border-radius` |\n\nThis is why setting `font-family` and `color` on `body` styles almost all the text, while a `border` on `body` does not draw a border around every child.\n\n### 3.10 Accessibility: make it work for everyone\n\n| Practice | Why it helps |\n|---|---|\n| Size text in `rem` and leave the root font size alone | People who need bigger text get it |\n| Test at 200% zoom and with a larger browser font setting | Text must stay readable with nothing cut off |\n| Use `min-height`, not a fixed `height`, on boxes with text | Prevents clipped text |\n| Use a plain-number `line-height` of about 1.5 | Easier to read, especially with dyslexia or low vision |\n| Keep contrast at 4.5 to 1 for normal text | People with low vision or in bright sunlight |\n| Do not use colour as the only clue | Colour-blind users |\n| Keep a visible focus outline on links and buttons | Keyboard users need to see where they are |\n| Switch off non-essential movement for people who ask: `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` | Avoids dizziness |\n| Make buttons and links big enough to tap (about 44 by 44 pixels) | Easier for fingers and for people with tremors |\n| Do not hide information with `display: none` or `opacity: 0` if people need it | Screen readers skip `display: none` content |\n\n### 3.11 Safety: things to watch out for\n\nCSS looks harmless, but text that visitors type can cause trouble if it ends up inside your CSS.\n\n| Risk | What could happen | Safe habit |\n|---|---|---|\n| **CSS injection** | A visitor's text is pasted into a `<style>` block or a `style` attribute and adds extra rules that hide warnings or fake buttons | Never paste raw visitor text into CSS |\n| **Letting visitors pick colours or sizes** | A value such as `red; } body { display: none` could break your page | Choose from a fixed list, or check the value first with `CSS.supports()` |\n| **`url()` values** | Any property that accepts `url(...)` can make the browser contact an address the attacker chose | Do not let visitors supply URLs for CSS |\n| **Outside fonts and stylesheets** | They can track visitors and change your page | Host your own files where you can |\n| **Hiding secrets with CSS** | `display: none` still sends the secret to the browser; anyone can see it | Keep secrets on the server |\n\nA safer way to apply a colour choice in JavaScript:\n\n```javascript\n// Risky: building CSS text from what a visitor typed\nstyleTag.textContent = \".banner { color: \" + userColour + \"; }\";\n\n// Safer: check the value, then set just one property\nif (CSS.supports(\"color\", userColour)) {\n  banner.style.setProperty(\"color\", userColour);\n}\n\n// Safest: choose from a fixed list\nconst allowed = { light: \"#0b3d5c\", dark: \"#8cc6ea\" };\nbanner.style.setProperty(\"color\", allowed[userChoice] || allowed.light);\n```\n\n### 3.12 Debugging: finding out what went wrong\n\nOpen the developer tools: press `F12`, or right-click the page and choose **Inspect**.\n\n| Problem | What to try |\n|---|---|\n| My style does nothing | In the **Styles** panel, look for a line with a **strikethrough** (another rule beat it) or a **warning triangle** (the value is invalid) |\n| What size is it really? | Open the **Computed** tab: it shows the final value in pixels |\n| Check a size with code | In the **Console**: `getComputedStyle($(\".box\")).width` |\n| A shorthand wiped out my other rule | In Styles, look for earlier lines crossed out by a later shorthand |\n| `calc()` fails | Missing spaces around `+` or `-` |\n| A variable is not working | Check the spelling and capital letters; check the name starts with `--` |\n| Sideways scrollbar appears | Something is wider than the screen, often `100vw` |\n| Text will not grow with the browser setting | It is sized in `px`, or the root font size was set in `px` |\n| Not sure a browser supports it | Check MDN's compatibility table, or test with `CSS.supports(\"width\", \"min(100%, 40rem)\")` |\n\n### 3.13 Common beginner mistakes\n\n| Mistake | Fix |\n|---|---|\n| `margin: 10;` (no unit) | `margin: 10px;` or `margin: 0.625rem;` |\n| `font-size: 1.5 rem;` (space) | `font-size: 1.5rem;` |\n| `calc(100%-2rem)` | `calc(100% - 2rem)` |\n| `border: 2px red;` (no style) | `border: 2px solid red;` |\n| `transition: 0.2` (no unit) | `transition: color 0.2s;` |\n| Font sizes in `px` | Use `rem` |\n| Nested `em` text growing out of control | Use `rem` for font sizes |\n| `line-height: 150%` | `line-height: 1.5` |\n| `width: 100vw` for \"full width\" | `width: 100%` |\n| Fixed `height` on a box of text | `min-height` |\n| Expecting `padding: 10%` to depend on height | It depends on the parent's **width** |\n| Using `opacity` to fade just a background | Use a colour with alpha: `rgb(0 0 0 / 50%)` |\n| Writing `font:` after `line-height:` and losing the line height | Put `line-height` after the `font` shorthand |\n| Pasting visitor text into CSS | Allow only a fixed list of choices |\n| Copying code without understanding it | Look up each property on MDN |\n\n**Part 3 summary.** You can use the main properties for size and space, text, colour, layout, and movement; choose units for each job; understand what is inherited; make CSS accessible; avoid injection risks; debug with the Computed tab and the Console; and avoid the usual beginner mistakes.\n\n---\n\n## Part 4 of 4: Check Your Understanding and Practice\n\n### Section A: Questions and answers\n\n**Q1. What is a CSS declaration, and what are its parts?**\n\n**Answer:** A declaration is one instruction made of a property (what to change), a colon, a value (what to change it to), and a semicolon, for example `color: blue;`. A rule is a selector plus a group of declarations in curly braces.\n\n**Q2. What does the browser do if it does not understand a value, such as `margin: 10;`?**\n\n**Answer:** It ignores that one declaration and carries on with the rest. Nothing breaks visibly, but nothing happens either, which is why a typo can be hard to spot. The fix is to write `margin: 10px;`, because sizes need a unit (except zero).\n\n**Q3. What is the order of the four values in `padding: 1rem 2rem 3rem 4rem;`?**\n\n**Answer:** Clockwise from the top: top 1rem, right 2rem, bottom 3rem, left 4rem.\n\n**Q4. Why does `border: 3px red;` show no border?**\n\n**Answer:** The border style was left out, and its default is `none`, so nothing is drawn. Write `border: 3px solid red;`.\n\n**Q5. Why is `rem` a better choice than `px` for font sizes?**\n\n**Answer:** `rem` is based on the root font size, which follows the visitor's browser text-size setting. If someone sets larger text, every `rem` size grows with it. Fixed `px` sizes ignore that choice, which hurts people who need larger text.\n\n**Q6. A parent has `font-size: 20px`. Each nested child has `font-size: 1.5em`. What are the first and second child's sizes, and what happens with `1.5rem` (root 16px)?**\n\n**Answer:** With `em`, each value is based on the parent's size, so the first child is 20 × 1.5 = 30px, and its child is 30 × 1.5 = 45px: the sizes multiply with each level. With `1.5rem`, every level is based on the root, so each is exactly 24px.\n\n**Q7. A parent is 400px wide. What is `padding: 10%` on a child inside it?**\n\n**Answer:** 40px on all four sides. Percentage padding (and margin) is calculated from the parent's **width**, even for the top and bottom.\n\n**Q8. Why should `line-height` be a plain number such as `1.5` instead of `150%`?**\n\n**Answer:** A plain number is passed to children as a multiplier, so each child calculates it from its own text size. A percentage is worked out on the parent and passed on as a fixed length, so a child with bigger text gets cramped, overlapping lines.\n\n**Q9. What does `font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 1rem + 3vw, 3rem)` do?**\n\n**Answer:** It aims for `1rem + 3vw` (which grows with the window width) but never goes below 1.75rem (28px) or above 3rem (48px). So the heading is 28px on narrow phones, grows smoothly on mid-sized screens, and stops at 48px on wide screens.\n\n**Q10. A page lets visitors choose a banner colour, and the code is `style.textContent = \".banner { color: \" + colour + \"; }\"`. What is the risk, and how do you fix it?**\n\n**Answer:** CSS injection: a visitor can type text that closes the rule and adds their own rules, for example to hide warnings or fake buttons. Instead, check the value with `CSS.supports(\"color\", colour)` and set just one property with `element.style.setProperty()`, or choose from a fixed list of allowed colours.\n\n### Section B: Practical (one task, step by step)\n\n**Goal:** build a small \"Values and Units Lab\" page for Harbor Goods. Each step adds one tiny experiment. You predict the result, check it with the browser's Console, and then break it on purpose so you can see what the errors look like.\n\n**What you need:** a computer, the Chrome, Edge, or Firefox browser, and a plain text editor (Notepad on Windows, TextEdit in plain text mode on Mac, or Visual Studio Code). Allow about 60 to 75 minutes.\n\n**Step 1: Create the project folder.**\n\n1. Open your **Documents** (or **Desktop**) folder.\n2. Create a new folder named `harbor-values`.\n3. Open that folder and create another folder inside it named `css`.\n\n**Step 2: Create the HTML file.**\n\n1. Open your text editor and create a new file.\n2. Choose **Save As** and save it as `index.html` inside the `harbor-values` folder (not inside `css`). Choose the **UTF-8** encoding if the Save window offers it.\n3. Make sure the file name ends in `.html` and not `.html.txt`. On Windows, if you cannot see file endings, open File Explorer, click **View**, and tick **File name extensions**.\n4. Paste this into the file, then save it (`Ctrl + S`, or `Cmd + S` on a Mac):\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n    <title>Values and Units Lab | Harbor Goods</title>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"css/style.css\">\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <header>\n      <h1>Values and Units Lab</h1>\n      <p>Harbor Goods style guide</p>\n    </header>\n\n    <main>\n      <section>\n        <h2>1. px or rem?</h2>\n        <p class=\"px-text\">This text is sized in px.</p>\n        <p class=\"rem-text\">This text is sized in rem.</p>\n      </section>\n\n      <section>\n        <h2>2. The em trap</h2>\n        <div class=\"em-box\">Level 1\n          <div class=\"em-box\">Level 2\n            <div class=\"em-box\">Level 3</div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </section>\n\n      <section>\n        <h2>3. Percentages</h2>\n        <div class=\"parent\">\n          <div class=\"child\">Child: width 50%, padding 10%</div>\n        </div>\n      </section>\n\n      <section>\n        <h2>4. Line height</h2>\n        <div class=\"lh-number\">Parent text 20px, line-height 1.5\n          <p class=\"big\">Child text 40px</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"lh-percent\">Parent text 20px, line-height 150%\n          <p class=\"big\">Child text 40px</p>\n        </div>\n      </section>\n\n      <section>\n        <h2>5. Fluid size</h2>\n        <p class=\"fluid-title\">Gear for life on the water</p>\n      </section>\n\n      <section>\n        <h2>6. Colours</h2>\n        <ul class=\"swatches\">\n          <li class=\"swatch hex\">hex</li>\n          <li class=\"swatch rgb\">rgb()</li>\n          <li class=\"swatch hsl\">hsl()</li>\n          <li class=\"swatch alpha\">alpha</li>\n        </ul>\n      </section>\n\n      <section>\n        <h2>7. Shorthand</h2>\n        <p class=\"short-padding\">Padding with four values</p>\n        <p class=\"short-border\">Border shorthand</p>\n        <p class=\"short-font\">Font shorthand</p>\n        <button type=\"button\" class=\"btn\">A button</button>\n      </section>\n    </main>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\n5. Go to the `harbor-values` folder and **double-click `index.html`**. It opens in your browser as plain, unstyled text. Leave this browser tab open.\n\n**How to see each change.** After every step below, do these three things: save the CSS file in your editor, switch to the browser tab, and press `F5` to refresh.\n\n**Step 3: Create the CSS file and test that it connects.**\n\n1. In your editor, create a new file and save it as `style.css` **inside the `css` folder**.\n2. Type this (typing it helps you learn it) and save:\n\n```css\n:root {\n  --blue: #0b3d5c;\n  --light: #f4f8fb;\n  --border: #d6e2ea;\n  --accent: #f2a900;\n}\n\n*, *::before, *::after {\n  box-sizing: border-box;\n}\n\nbody {\n  margin: 0 auto;\n  max-width: 50rem;\n  padding: 1rem;\n  font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;\n  line-height: 1.6;\n  color: #1c1c1c;\n  background: var(--light);\n}\n\nh1, h2 {\n  color: var(--blue);\n  line-height: 1.25;\n}\n\nh2 {\n  margin-top: 2rem;\n  font-size: 1.4rem;\n}\n```\n\n3. Refresh the browser. You should see a centred column on a pale background, with blue headings.\n4. **If nothing changed:** the stylesheet is not connected. Check that the file is `css/style.css` (the `css` folder spelled exactly like that) and that the `<link>` line in the HTML says `href=\"css/style.css\"`.\n5. Now open the developer tools: press `F12` (or right-click the page and choose **Inspect**), and click the **Console** tab. Keep it open for the rest of the practical. This is a place where you can type small bits of code and see answers.\n\n**Step 4: px compared with rem.**\n\n1. Add this to the **end** of `style.css`:\n\n```css\n.px-text  { font-size: 16px; }\n.rem-text { font-size: 1rem; }\n```\n\n2. Save and refresh. The two lines look the same.\n3. In the Console, type this and press `Enter`:\n\n```javascript\n[getComputedStyle($(\".px-text\")).fontSize, getComputedStyle($(\".rem-text\")).fontSize]\n```\n\nYou should see `[\"16px\", \"16px\"]`.\n\n4. Now change your browser's default text size:\n   - **Chrome:** click the three dots, then **Settings**, then **Appearance**, find **Font size**, and choose **Large**.\n   - **Edge:** **Settings**, then **Appearance**, then **Fonts**, and change the **Font size** to **Large**.\n   - **Firefox:** **Settings**, then **General**, scroll to **Fonts**, click **Advanced**, and set **Default font size** to `20`.\n5. Go back to your page, refresh, and run the Console line again.\n\n| Element | What you should see |\n|---|---|\n| `.px-text` | Still 16px (it ignored your setting) |\n| `.rem-text` | Bigger, about 20px (it followed your setting) |\n\n6. Write one sentence in a notepad about why `rem` helps people who need larger text.\n7. **Put your browser's text size back to the normal default** (Medium in Chrome and Edge, 16 in Firefox) and refresh, so the numbers in the next steps match.\n\n**Step 5: The em trap.**\n\n1. Add to the end of `style.css`:\n\n```css\n.em-box {\n  font-size: 1.2em;\n  padding: 0.5em;\n  margin: 0.5em 0;\n  background: #e5f1f8;\n  border: 1px solid var(--border);\n}\n```\n\n2. Save and refresh. You will see three boxes inside each other, with bigger text each time.\n3. **Predict first.** Write in your notepad what you think the three text sizes are. Then run this in the Console:\n\n```javascript\n$$(\".em-box\").map(el => getComputedStyle(el).fontSize)\n```\n\nYou should see `[\"19.2px\", \"23.04px\", \"27.648px\"]`. Each box is 1.2 times its parent: 16 × 1.2 = 19.2, then 19.2 × 1.2 = 23.04, and so on.\n\n4. Now change `font-size: 1.2em;` to `font-size: 1.2rem;`, save, refresh, and run the Console line again. You should see `[\"19.2px\", \"19.2px\", \"19.2px\"]`. With `rem`, the sizes do not multiply. You can change it back to `em` if you want to see the trap again.\n\n**Step 6: What percentages mean.**\n\n1. Add:\n\n```css\n.parent {\n  width: 25rem;\n  max-width: 100%;\n  background: #e5f1f8;\n}\n\n.child {\n  width: 50%;\n  padding: 10%;\n  background: #ffffff;\n  border: 1px solid var(--blue);\n}\n```\n\n2. Save and refresh. **Make your browser window wide** (wider than about 500 pixels), or the parent will shrink and your numbers will be different.\n3. The parent is 25rem, which is 400px. **Predict:** how wide is the child, and how much padding does it have at the top and on the left? Then run:\n\n```javascript\nconst c = $(\".child\");\n[getComputedStyle(c).width, getComputedStyle(c).paddingTop, getComputedStyle(c).paddingLeft]\n```\n\nYou should see `[\"200px\", \"40px\", \"40px\"]`. Notice that the **top** padding is also 40px, because percentage padding is based on the parent's **width**, not its height.\n\n4. Change `.parent` to `width: 20rem;`, save, refresh, and run the line again. You should see `[\"160px\", \"32px\", \"32px\"]`. Change it back to `25rem`.\n\n**Step 7: Line height, number or percent.**\n\n1. Add:\n\n```css\n.lh-number,\n.lh-percent {\n  font-size: 20px;\n  margin-bottom: 1rem;\n  background: #e5f1f8;\n}\n\n.lh-number  { line-height: 1.5; }\n.lh-percent { line-height: 150%; }\n\n.big {\n  margin: 0;\n  font-size: 40px;\n  background: #ffffff;\n}\n```\n\n2. Save and refresh. Look at the two \"Child text 40px\" lines. The first has comfortable spacing, and the second looks squashed.\n3. Run in the Console:\n\n```javascript\n$$(\".big\").map(el => getComputedStyle(el).lineHeight)\n```\n\nYou should see `[\"60px\", \"30px\"]`. The number (1.5) was passed down and used with the child's 40px text: 40 × 1.5 = 60. The percentage was turned into 30px (20 × 1.5) on the parent, and the child inherited that fixed 30px, which is too small for 40px text.\n\n4. Write one sentence: which form of line height will you use from now on?\n\n**Step 8: A fluid heading with `clamp()`.**\n\n1. Add:\n\n```css\n.fluid-title {\n  margin: 0;\n  font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 1rem + 3vw, 3rem);\n  font-weight: 700;\n  color: var(--blue);\n}\n```\n\n2. Save and refresh. Now **drag the edge of your browser window** to make it narrower and wider. The heading grows and shrinks smoothly.\n3. Run this in the Console at four different window widths (resize between each run):\n\n```javascript\n[innerWidth, getComputedStyle($(\".fluid-title\")).fontSize]\n```\n\n4. Fill in this table with what you see:\n\n| Window width | Expected size | What you measured |\n|---|---|---|\n| About 400px | 28px (the smallest allowed) | |\n| About 600px | About 34px | |\n| About 1000px | About 46px | |\n| 1400px or wider | 48px (the largest allowed) | |\n\n**Step 9: Colours written in different ways.**\n\n1. Add:\n\n```css\n.swatches {\n  display: flex;\n  flex-wrap: wrap;\n  gap: 0.5rem;\n  margin: 0;\n  padding: 0;\n  list-style: none;\n}\n\n.swatch {\n  padding: 1rem 1.5rem;\n  color: #ffffff;\n  border-radius: 0.5rem;\n}\n\n.hex   { background: #0b3d5c; }\n.rgb   { background: rgb(11 61 92); }\n.hsl   { background: hsl(203 78.7% 20.2%); }\n.alpha { background: rgb(11 61 92 / 50%); color: #000000; }\n```\n\n2. Save and refresh. The first three swatches should look the same, and the fourth is lighter because it is half see-through.\n3. Run in the Console:\n\n```javascript\n$$(\".swatch\").map(el => getComputedStyle(el).backgroundColor)\n```\n\nYou should see three copies of `\"rgb(11, 61, 92)\"` and one `\"rgba(11, 61, 92, 0.5)\"`. (The `hsl` one may differ by 1 in one number because of rounding. That is normal.)\n\n4. **Break it on purpose:** change `#0b3d5c` in the `.hex` rule to `#0b3d5` (five digits instead of six). Save and refresh. The swatch loses its colour. Right-click it, choose **Inspect**, and look in the **Styles** panel for a warning triangle or a crossed-out line. Put the correct six digits back.\n5. In the Styles panel, click the small coloured square beside any colour. A picker opens that shows the **contrast ratio**. Check whether the black text on the \"alpha\" swatch reaches 4.5.\n\n**Step 10: Shorthand traps.**\n\n1. Add:\n\n```css\n.short-padding {\n  padding: 0.25rem 1rem 0.5rem 2rem;\n  background: #e5f1f8;\n}\n\n.short-border {\n  padding: 0.5rem;\n  border: 3px solid var(--accent);\n}\n\n.short-font {\n  line-height: 3;\n  font: italic 700 1.1rem Georgia, serif;\n}\n```\n\n2. Save and refresh.\n3. **The four-value order.** Run in the Console:\n\n```javascript\nconst p = getComputedStyle($(\".short-padding\"));\n[p.paddingTop, p.paddingRight, p.paddingBottom, p.paddingLeft]\n```\n\nYou should see `[\"4px\", \"16px\", \"8px\", \"32px\"]`. That is top, right, bottom, left, clockwise.\n\n4. **Border with no style.** In `.short-border`, delete the word `solid` so it reads `border: 3px var(--accent);`. Save and refresh. The border disappears! Put `solid` back.\n5. **The font shorthand wipes line-height.** In the `.short-font` rule, `line-height: 3` comes *before* the `font` shorthand. Run:\n\n```javascript\ngetComputedStyle($(\".short-font\")).lineHeight\n```\n\nYou should see `\"normal\"`, not 3. Right-click the paragraph, choose **Inspect**, and notice `line-height: 3` is crossed out in the Styles panel. Now move the `line-height: 3;` line to **below** the `font` line, save, refresh, and run the Console line again. This time you should see a number close to `\"52.8px\"` (3 × 17.6px).\n\n**Step 11: A button, `inherit`, and a transition.**\n\n1. Add:\n\n```css\n.btn {\n  padding: 0.6rem 1.25rem;\n  color: #1c1c1c;\n  background: var(--accent);\n  border: 0;\n  border-radius: 0.5rem;\n  cursor: pointer;\n}\n```\n\n2. Save and refresh. The button's text looks smaller and a different style than the rest of the page, because buttons do not copy the page's font. Run:\n\n```javascript\ngetComputedStyle($(\".btn\")).fontSize\n```\n\nYou will probably see `\"13.3333px\"`.\n\n3. Add `font: inherit;` as the **first** line inside the `.btn { ... }` braces. Save, refresh, and run the Console line again. You should now see `\"16px\"`, and the button text matches the page.\n4. Now add a smooth colour change. Inside `.btn { ... }` add `transition: background-color 0.2s ease;`, and after the closing brace add:\n\n```css\n.btn:hover { background: #ffc233; }\n\n.btn:focus-visible {\n  outline: 3px solid var(--blue);\n  outline-offset: 3px;\n}\n```\n\n5. Save and refresh. Hover over the button and watch the colour fade. Press the `Tab` key until the button shows a dark outline: that is the focus ring that keyboard users depend on.\n6. **Break it on purpose:** change `0.2s` to `0.2` (no unit). Save, refresh, and hover. The smooth change stops, because a time needs a unit. Look for a warning in the Styles panel. Put `0.2s` back.\n\n**Step 12: Custom properties (variables).**\n\n1. In `:root`, change `--accent: #f2a900;` to `--accent: #2e9e6b;`. Save and refresh. Everything that used `--accent` (the button and the border) turns green at once. Change it back.\n2. Add this temporary rule and refresh:\n\n```css\n.btn { --accent: crimson; }\n```\n\nOnly the button turns red, because the variable was given a new value just for that element. Delete the rule.\n\n3. Add this and refresh:\n\n```css\n.child { border-top: 4px solid var(--missing, hotpink); }\n```\n\nThe top border is pink, because `--missing` does not exist, so the fallback after the comma is used. Delete the rule.\n\n4. In `:root`, change `--light: #f4f8fb;` to `--light: banana;`. Save and refresh. The page background loses its colour, because `banana` is not a colour, so the whole `background` property stops working. Change it back.\n5. Run this in the Console:\n\n```javascript\ngetComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue(\"--blue\")\n```\n\nYou should see `\" #0b3d5c\"` (possibly with a space at the front).\n\n**Step 13: Break things on purpose (one at a time).**\n\nMake each change in `style.css`, refresh, look at what happens and at the **Styles** panel, and then undo it:\n\n1. Give `.child` the line `margin: 10;` (no unit). Does it work? (No: the line is ignored.)\n2. Change `.rem-text` to `font-size: 1.5 rem;` (with a space). The line is ignored.\n3. Give `.child` the line `padding: 1rem 2rem 3rem 4rem 5rem;` (five values). It is ignored because `padding` takes at most four values.\n4. Add the line `width: calc(100%-2rem);` to `.parent`. It is ignored because the spaces around `-` are missing.\n5. Add `width: 100vw;` to `.parent` and look for a sideways scrollbar. Then change it back to `25rem`.\n6. Add this rule at the end: `html { font-size: 16px; }`. Change your browser text size to Large again (as in Step 4) and check that the `rem` text **no longer grows**. This shows why you should not set the root size in pixels. Delete the rule and put your browser text size back to normal.\n\n**Step 14: Look inside with developer tools.**\n\n1. Right-click the `.child` box and choose **Inspect**.\n2. Click the **Computed** tab. Find `padding-top`, and click the small arrow beside it to see which rule it came from.\n3. Look at the **box model diagram** (the coloured nested rectangles). Find the content, padding, border, and margin numbers.\n4. Go back to the **Styles** tab, tick the box beside `padding: 10%` off and on, and watch the box change.\n5. In the Console, check what your browser supports:\n\n```javascript\n[CSS.supports(\"width\", \"min(100%, 40rem)\"), CSS.supports(\"color\", \"banana\")]\n```\n\nYou should see `[true, false]`.\n\n**Step 15: Check accessibility.**\n\n1. Change your browser's text size to **Very large** (or `24` in Firefox) and refresh. Scroll through the page. Does any text overlap or get cut off? Write down what you see, then put the setting back to normal.\n2. Zoom the page to 200% (`Ctrl` and `+`, or `Cmd` and `+`). Does everything still read well, with no sideways scrolling?\n3. Open the **Lighthouse** tab in developer tools, tick **Accessibility**, and click **Analyze page load**. Read any problems it lists, and fix at least one.\n4. Press `Tab` from the top of the page and check that the button shows a clear focus ring.\n\n**Step 16: Validate your files.**\n\n1. Go to `jigsaw.w3.org/css-validator`, click the **By direct input** tab, paste all of `style.css`, and click **Check**. Read each message. (Some warnings may mention very new features, so decide whether each one is a real mistake.)\n2. Go to `validator.w3.org`, click **Validate by Direct Input**, paste all of `index.html`, and click **Check**.\n3. Fix any real errors and check again.\n\n**Step 17: Safety check.**\n\nAnswer these in your notepad:\n\n1. Does any visitor's text end up inside your CSS? (It should not.)\n2. If you added a \"pick your colour\" box, what would you do before using the value? (Check it with `CSS.supports()`, set only one property with `style.setProperty()`, or choose from a fixed list.)\n3. Does your page load any font or stylesheet from another website? If yes, what could go wrong?\n4. Why is `display: none` not a safe way to hide a secret?\n\n**Step 18: Reflect.**\n\nAnswer these in your notepad, in one or two lines each:\n\n1. Which unit will you now use for font sizes, line height, thin borders, and page width, and why?\n2. What is a percentage always a percentage \"of\"?\n3. Why did the `font` shorthand remove your `line-height`, and how can you avoid that?\n4. What is the difference between a value the browser throws away straight away (like `margin: 10`) and a variable that holds a bad value?\n5. Which step surprised you most?\n\n**Finished check.** You should now have: a project with `index.html` and `css/style.css`; proof that `px` text ignores the browser's text-size setting while `rem` follows it; the em sizes 19.2px, 23.04px, and 27.648px; 40px of padding in a 400px parent; line heights of 60px and 30px; four `clamp()` heading sizes from 28px to 48px; three colour formats that all show `rgb(11, 61, 92)`; the four-value padding order, the missing border style trap, and the `font` shorthand reset; a button that uses `font: inherit`, a transition, and a visible focus ring; custom properties with a fallback; every deliberate mistake reproduced and undone; and clean validator results.\n\n### Self-check\n\n| Skill | Done |\n|---|---|\n| Name the parts of a declaration (property, value, semicolon) | ☐ |\n| Explain what happens when a value is wrong | ☐ |\n| Look up a property on MDN | ☐ |\n| Use `inherit` and `initial` | ☐ |\n| Read shorthand and the clockwise four-value order | ☐ |\n| Avoid the three shorthand traps | ☐ |\n| Write values correctly (units, spaces, times) | ☐ |\n| Choose between `px`, `rem`, `em`, `%`, `vw`, `ch`, `fr` | ☐ |\n| Explain the `em` trap | ☐ |\n| Say what each percentage is a percentage of | ☐ |\n| Use a plain-number `line-height` | ☐ |\n| Write `calc()`, `min()`, `max()`, and `clamp()` | ☐ |\n| Write colours as names, hex, `rgb()`, `hsl()`, and with transparency | ☐ |\n| Check colour contrast | ☐ |\n| Use custom properties and fallbacks | ☐ |\n| Use the box model and `box-sizing: border-box` | ☐ |\n| Use text, layout, and transition properties | ☐ |\n| Know which properties are inherited | ☐ |\n| Make CSS accessible (rem text, contrast, focus rings, reduced motion) | ☐ |\n| Avoid CSS injection | ☐ |\n| Debug with the Computed tab and the Console | ☐ |\n| Validate CSS and HTML | ☐ |\n\n**Lesson complete.**";
